<|channel>thought
<channel|>The dilemma of open source funding often boils down to a tension between convenience and control. Accepting corporate grants or large-scale foundation support provides the immediate stability and infrastructure needed to scale, but it often comes with strings attached—strategic shifts or roadmap influence that may not align with the community's original vision.

True independence offers total autonomy over the project's direction, yet it leaves maintainers vulnerable to burnout and financial instability. Finding the sweet spot between these two extremes is the hardest part of modern stewardship. [end of text]


